  disjoint seeds locksat-rep 24 pairs  17W/7L  median -8.27%  p=0.0639
  disjoint inst  locksat-tr  24 pairs  18W/3L  median -8.51%  p=0.0015
  preliminary    locksat-p4  12 pairs   6W/6L  median +0.84%  p=1.0000

Pooled 52W/17L across three sets whose medians agree at -8.3 to -8.7%. The
replication discipline is satisfied on both axes that matter: disjoint seeds and
disjoint instances.

Preliminary is safe by construction, not by luck: P3 (sat 0.464) locks under
both thresholds, P5 has 4 bays and P6 sits at 1.514 so neither is a spec board,
and P4 -- the only one that flips -- measured a dead tie at 6W/6L.

Residual risk, recorded: train_final prob_9 (sat 0.760) loses in BOTH gates
(+4.43%, +6.61%), so the loss is real and not noise, and no saturation cut
separates it from the winners at 0.636/0.689/0.836. The band is taken whole or
not at all. Expected value is clearly positive; it is not free.

v6.4.1 (2026-08-14) = v6.4 + floor availability repair (REGRESSION FIX).

v6.4's reservation moves entry days, which changes the seed board -- and on P5
the new seed carries checker violations the gate ladder cannot clear. Measured:
the start-of-run floor failed on 4 of 4 seeds there, against v6.3's 4 of 4
successes. That put P5 back on the `_trivial` path (1,321,314,754, 124x the
median) that v6.2 exists to remove, on the very board where that catastrophe
was originally measured. v6.2's "available 32/32" was measured against v6.1's
seed; adopting anything that changes the seed invalidates it, and v6.4's
adoption review did not recheck it.

`_make_floor` tried exactly one construction and gave up after 0.4s of a 20s
budget. The floor only has to be CHECKER-CLEAN, not good, so it now tries up to
four distinct constructions and keeps the first that gates (a rebuild costs
~0.15s). Restored 16/16 across four boards x four seeds; P5's catastrophe
return goes 1,321,314,754 -> 19,409,115, a factor of 68.

v6.4 (2026-08-13) = v6.3 + the energetic reservation, repaired and load-gated.

Defect: `knobs::reserve()` has been on since v5 and never once fired. Its
threshold was `fillRatio`, which is `min(1, max(fill, max_t dayLoad))` by
construction, so `dayLoad[t] > thr` cannot be true; and the demand profile
counted one area-unit per day of [R, D-P], peaking at 0.19-0.54 of capacity
against a real occupancy of 58%. The pathology it was written to counter is in
the kernel's own comment: insertGreedy always takes the earliest legal day, so
slack-rich blocks occupy prime early space and tight blocks find none -- at 63%
utilisation, with tardy blocks waiting 13-19 days past release.

Fix: a second, residency-aware demand profile (area * P / span over [R, D], the
standard energetic profile), thresholded against `packFill`, the density the
instance can actually pack. `fillRatio` and `dayLoad` are untouched, so the
density screen at repair.hpp:102 keeps its shipped input.

Gated by peak load. Free deferral is not uniformly good: measured across 35
train_final instances it is worth -5.09% below peak load 1.60 and turns harmful
above 2.2, where most days exceed the threshold, the scan walks to D-P without
finding relief, and -- since every insertGreedy phase starts at eBot -- the
block loses its early days for nothing. Above 1.60 the guard stands down.

